[
  "Welcome back to The Sound Above. I am Mike Hall. Today we enter Philosophy, Empathy & Craftsmanship. Before code synthesis models and automated agents, developers met face to face to establish standards of craftsmanship. Today we rewatch Episode 23: Adewale Oshineye from SCNA 2013 (2013).",
  "Ade traveled to SCNA Chicago to share the foundational lessons of 'Apprenticeship Patterns', co-authored with Dave Hoover.",
  "Who were the mentors who shaped Ade Oshineye? What did he learn across Google and international software communities about passing the torch to the next generation? What principles guided Adewale Oshineye when this conversation was recorded? How do those lessons inform the software systems we design today?",
  "Roll archive footage (YouTube ID: GGhUZTBA6L4). Watch for the unvarnished hallway exchanges, the emphasis on craft over speed, and the sheer enthusiasm of engineers taking responsibility for their software.",
  "Apprenticeship is how unspoken tacit knowledge survives technological interregnums. When models generate solutions, understanding 'why' requires human lineage. When implementation is cheap, verification and clarity become everything. We cannot accept the mirage of competence. Let us examine the terminal contracts directly.",
  "Thank you for joining today's session. Full transcripts, provenance notes, and companion research are available at just3ws.com. In our next broadcast, we explore Episode 24. Until next time, keep your feedback loops fast and your code humane."
]